# Source: Exercise sheet "Hoare Logic 2" (solved), 2 d), and HoareLogic.pdf, "Empty else-branch".
id: empty-else
title: A conditional with a skip branch
topic: Conditional
level: 2
source: Exercise sheet "Hoare Logic 2", 2 d; HoareLogic.pdf, "Empty else-branch"
tags: conditional, skip, sequence

task:
  Prove that the program sets $z$ to $0$ when $x = 0$, and to $1$
  otherwise.

pre: true
post: (x = 0 && z = 0) || (x != 0 && z = 1)

program:
  {P}
  {R2}
  z := 1;             // i1
  {R1}
  if (x = 0) then {
    z := 0            // then
  } else {
    skip              // else
  }
  {Q}

verdict: valid

step: The then branch, `z := 0`
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: then
calc:
  wp(z := 0, Q)
  = (x = 0 && 0 = 0) || (x != 0 && 0 = 1)
  = x = 0 || false
  = x = 0

step: The else branch, `skip`
rule: SKIP, WP
focus: else
calc:
  wp(skip, Q)
  = Q
text:
  The branch does nothing, so whatever must hold after the conditional
  must already hold before it.

step: Combine the branches
rule: IF, WP
focus: if
calc:
  wp(if (x = 0) then z := 0 else skip, Q)
  = (x = 0 ==> x = 0) && (!(x = 0) ==> (x = 0 && z = 0) || (x != 0 && z = 1))
  = true && (x != 0 ==> z = 1)       // when x != 0, only the second disjunct can hold
  = x != 0 ==> z = 1
set: R1 = x != 0 ==> z = 1
text:
  This is the condition of the empty branch that the reference document
  writes as $P && !B ==> Q$: when the condition is false, the
  postcondition must already be true.

step: The first statement, `z := 1`
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: i1
calc:
  wp(z := 1, R1)
  = (x != 0 ==> z = 1)[z <- 1]
  = x != 0 ==> 1 = 1
  = true
set: R2 = true
vc: true ==> true
why:
  The precondition is $true$ and the weakest precondition is $true$.
result: The triple is valid.

summary:
  The assignment `z := 1` before the conditional is what the empty else
  branch relies on: when $x != 0$ nothing changes $z$ any more, so it must
  already be $1$.
